{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["R6", "R9"],
      "pins": ["J1.2", "J1.3", "M1A.IO1", "M1A.IO0"],
      "nets": ["FLOAT_HIGH", "FLOAT_LOW", "+3V3", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the root sheet (Water Level Sensor/Switches block), R6 (10 kΩ) is connected as a pull-UP from +3V3 to the FLOAT_HIGH net (J1 pin 2, which SW1 shorts to GND), while R9 (10 kΩ) is connected as a pull-DOWN from the FLOAT_LOW net (J1 pin 3) to GND. Since SW2 also connects FLOAT_LOW to GND, the FLOAT_LOW line is held at 0 V regardless of water level and the float switch has no effect.",
      "expected": "The specification's reference design has the pull resistors the other way round: 'R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k' (§4.4) — i.e. R6 is the pull-up on FLOAT_LOW (so the low-level float, wired to GND, can pull the line low) and R9 is the pull-down on FLOAT_HIGH.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "matching ... the netlist's FLOAT_LOW/FLOAT_HIGH nets (with 10 kΩ/4.7 kΩ pull resistors visible in the .cir file: R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k, R2 FLOAT_LOW /BPUMP 4.7k, R11 FLOAT_HIGH /BATO 4.7k)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1, 'Safety Switches': +3V3 → R6 10k → node labeled FLOAT_HIGH (J1 pin 2, SW1 to GND); node labeled FLOAT_LOW (J1 pin 3, SW2 to GND) → R9 10k → GND"}
      ],
      "impact": "The low-water float interlock is dead: FLOAT_LOW never transitions, so the hardware path through R2/T1 that is supposed to de-energize the main circulation pump on low level never activates. The pump can run dry, defeating the low-level pump-cutoff safety feature the spec describes.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    },
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["PR1", "H1", "P1"],
      "pins": ["PR1.VCC", "PR1.SDA", "PR1.SCL", "PR1.GND"],
      "nets": ["I2C_SDA", "I2C_SCL", "+5V", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the root sheet ('Water Height Sensor'), the TF-Luna LiDAR PR1 is wired through header H1 to the main I2C bus (+5V, GND, I2C_SDA, I2C_SCL) with decoupling C1 100 µF.",
      "expected": "The specification states PR1 is documentation-only in r2: 'all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design' (§4.3, §6).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no, in_bom: yes, and all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1, 'Water Level Sensor/Switches → Water Height Sensor': PR1 LiDAR pins VCC/SDA/SCL/GND wired to H1 PB_1.25mm carrying +5V, GND, I2C_SDA, I2C_SCL"}
      ],
      "impact": "This build connects a sensor the intended design leaves unconnected; if populated, the LiDAR loads the +5V rail and the I2C bus (and an EZO_PDIS label also appears on H1 pin 5), though firmware does not address it. Minor behavioral deviation compared with the reference design.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The power train, I2C/isolation architecture, TMC2209 drivers, and pump/valve switches match the specification, but the float-switch pull resistors are swapped relative to the reference design (R6 pull-up on FLOAT_HIGH, R9 pull-down on FLOAT_LOW), which leaves the low-water float interlock unable to assert and disables the low-level pump cutoff; the LiDAR is also wired to the I2C bus contrary to the spec's statement that it is unconnected."
}

